WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.960
Imagine trying to run a health care system for like 1.3 million people.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.880
Yeah. That's a massive undertaking.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.800
Right. You have to hire specialists. You're negotiating these multi-year contracts with

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:19.120
providers and securing massive supply chains for clinics and hospitals.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.040
And you need stability to do any of that.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.160
Exactly. But now imagine trying to make all of those long-term structural decisions

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:33.840
knowing that a huge chunk of your operating budget might just, wow, vanish in exactly 12 months.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:34.960
Right. Now 13 months.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:35.360
12.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.440
Yeah. You don't have a 13-month runway.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:44.240
There's no vague timeline here. You have a hard, unforgiving cliff set for September 30th, 2027.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.000
And I mean, the pressure on the ground in that scenario just fundamentally distorts

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:48.960
how care is delivered.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:49.440
Oh, absolutely.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.600
Because when administrators can't guarantee funding past that 12-month horizon,

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.720
while preventative care initiatives stall out, capital investments in medical technology get

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:03.760
completely frozen and retaining top-tier medical talent, I mean, that becomes nearly impossible

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.240
because the doctors know the money might just run out.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.600
Exactly. We are looking at a situation where the foundational health coverage for American citizens

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:17.840
in Puerto Rico is resting on this legislative fault line that is scheduled to crack wide open.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.240
So welcome to the deep dive.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.600
Our mission for you today is to pull apart this really complex web of federal financing,

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.720
strict congressional timelines, and local executive mandates.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.000
Because it really is a maze.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.880
It is. We want to understand this incredibly intense 12-month legislative sprint that is

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.840
kicking off right now. Puerto Rico is trying to secure a permanent Medicaid funding and,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.440
you know, they have to navigate a lot of political hurdles to actually get it done.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.280
Yeah. And to really grasp the magnitude of this 12-month sprint,

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.240
we need to first look at the structural disparity in how the federal government treats

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.080
Puerto Rico compared to the 50 states. Right, because it's not a level playing field.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.800
Not at all. In the states, Medicaid funding is open-ended. It scales with need.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:08.480
So if a state faces a sudden economic downturn, or like a public health emergency,

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.840
and thousands of new residents suddenly qualify for Medicaid,

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.840
the federal matching funds just flow automatically to cover that surge.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:16.800
There's no ceiling.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.240
But for Puerto Rico, the whole architecture of the funding is entirely different.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.880
They operate under a hard statutory cap. Yes.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:26.480
Once they hit a specific dollar amount, the federal tap just turns off.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.080
It doesn't matter how many eligible citizens still need care.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.160
And it's not just the cap either. It's the federal medical assistance percentage,

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.240
the FMP. Right, the FMP.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.880
The permanent statutory FMAP for Puerto Rico sits at just 55%.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.800
Which is incredibly low.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:48.480
It is. It means the federal government only covers 55% of the costs up to that artificial cap,

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.760
and the local territorial government is left to shoulder the rest.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.640
And over the years, I mean, Congress has acknowledged that this 55% baseline is just

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.000
untenable. But instead of fixing the underlying law, they just rely on temporary patches.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.760
Right, the classic band-aid approach.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.240
Yeah. And the most recent patch, which was wrapped into the Consolidated Appropriations

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.640
Act of 2023, the CAA that bumped the federal match up to 76%.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.760
But if you're listening to this and you follow territorial policy,

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.040
you already know the danger of this patch system.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:24.080
It creates a perpetual cycle of lobbying and, honestly, anxiety.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:31.040
Exactly. And the kicker with the CAA 2023 patch is that it expires on September 30th,

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:32.080
2027.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.200
So if Congress does absolutely nothing over the next 12 months,

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:39.840
the system just snaps back to that devastating 55% statutory limit.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:45.680
Which would be catastrophic. But securing a permanent fix requires way more than just

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.680
pointing out the inequity of the situation.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.840
Right. Capitol Hill operates on a very specific currency when it comes to these

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.760
massive legislative battles. You need an indisputable shared reality

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.040
before any committee chair will even think about drafting a bill.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.560
That brings us to the Congressional Research Service, the CRS.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:08.320
Yes, the CRS. I always like to think of them as the indisputable referee's rule book in a highly

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:09.520
politicized game.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:14.320
That is a great analogy, actually. Because in an environment where every think tank

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.520
and lobbying firm is pushing their own specialized data, the CRS acts as the

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:21.360
ultimate nonpartisan referee.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.120
They don't lobby. They don't advocate.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.400
Right. They just meticulously document current law.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:32.240
If a fact is published in the CRS report, it essentially becomes legislative canon.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:36.320
And the principal author of that canon for territorial Medicaid is Allison Mitchell.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:43.280
Yes. Allison Mitchell's reports are literally the foundational documents for this entire 12-month strategy.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:46.400
Because think about the reality of a congressional office.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:51.280
You have a 20-something legislative assistant handling 10 different policy portfolios.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:55.360
Oh, they're completely swamped. They are flooded with hundreds of pages of partisan

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.200
lobbying material every single day.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.600
Right. They do not have the time to sit down and read a 300-page historical analysis

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.120
from some advocacy group.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.680
No. They read Allison Mitchell's two-page staff briefs. That's what they actually rely on.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:11.760
And the specific CRS documents she authored are just fascinating.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.320
Because they don't just explain the law. They highlight the glaring contradictions in it.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.760
Like document R47601.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:25.600
Yes. The definitive statute by statute legislative history tracking everything from 1965

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.840
through the CAA 2023.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:33.520
And then there's the IF11012 brief, which is the crucial two-pager the staffers actually read.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:37.040
But maybe the most important one is R49126.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:38.400
Why that one specifically?

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:44.640
Because that's the document that explicitly flags the 2026 to 2027 Puerto Rico Medicaid

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.240
expiration as a congressional must-act item.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.520
Ah, so it forces the issue. It officially documents the cap funding and that impending

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:54.880
12-month cliff.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.560
Exactly. But more importantly, for Puerto Rico's leverage,

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:02.320
Allison Mitchell's reports clearly outlined the disparity between Puerto Rico and the

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:03.840
other four U.S. territories.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.320
Right. This is a huge point. Guam, the U.S. Virgin Islands, American Samoa,

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:12.560
and the Northern Mariana Islands, they do not face this 2027 cliff.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:14.560
Because Congress already gave them permanence.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:15.440
We really?

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:21.840
Yeah. Back in 2022, Congress granted those four territories a permanent 83% federal match.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:25.680
Wow. But in that exact same legislative package,

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.480
Puerto Rico was carved out and only given a temporary five-year patch at 76%.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:37.360
So why? What was the political rationale for treating Puerto Rico so differently in that

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:38.560
exact same moment?

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:40.400
It really just comes down to scale and cost.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:46.320
Granting a permanent 83% FMAP to American Samoa, for example,

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:48.720
involves a relatively small federal outlay.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:50.960
Right. The CBO score for that is tiny.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.600
It's easily absorbed into a larger spending package.

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:58.400
Exactly. But Puerto Rico, with its 1.3 million Medicaid enrollees,

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:01.520
represents a multi-billion-dollar federal commitment.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:04.960
So Congress took the politically easier path in 2022.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:08.880
They solved the cheap problem permanently and just kicked the expensive problem five

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:09.760
years down the road.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:12.880
And now we are at the end of that road. The 12-month sprint is here.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:15.280
Okay. So the CRS documents provide the baseline.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:19.040
They prove the cap exists. They prove the cliff is 12 months away.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:24.560
And they prove the other territories already have that permanent 83% match that Puerto Rico wants.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:29.600
Right. But having the facts on your side in Washington doesn't automatically unlock billions

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:30.400
of dollars.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:31.280
Unfortunately not.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:34.960
Historically, the pushback against giving Puerto Rico permanent funding

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:37.840
always circles back to questions of program integrity.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:41.440
Right. The standard character argument from fiscal conservatives is always about

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:46.720
administrative capacity. Can the local government actually manage a permanently expanded

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:50.160
federal budget without waste or mismanagement?

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.400
Which means the narrative for this 12-month sprint has to shift.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.040
It has to move away from just what the federal government owes

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:01.120
and focus entirely on what Puerto Rico has definitively proven it can do.

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:03.200
And this is where doing the homework comes in.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:07.280
The requirements of that 2023 CAA patch.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:11.760
Because Congress didn't just hand over a temporary 76% FMAP, right?

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:15.360
No, not at all. They attached incredibly strict compliance strings to it.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:17.280
Congress basically set a test.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:18.480
A massive test.

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:22.800
Yeah. They man-doted that Puerto Rico overhauled its program integrity structure

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.640
to justify those temporary funds.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:28.720
And Puerto Rico responded by entirely restructuring their oversight.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:30.480
So what did they actually do?

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:34.240
Well, for starters, they designated a specialized program integrity lead.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.480
And a separate contracting and procurement oversight lead.

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:40.800
Okay. But let's look at how that actually functions.

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:44.320
Because, you know, it can't just be about changing titles on an org chart.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:48.400
We are talking about fundamentally changing how billions of dollars are monitored.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:53.360
Exactly. And the best example of that is the asset verification system, the AVS.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:56.640
CMS actually approved Puerto Rico's AVS, right?

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:01.920
They did. And the AVS implementation is a massive technological hurdle.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:03.520
I mean, many states struggle with this,

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:06.880
let alone a territory operating under the stress of a temporary budget.

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:08.960
So how did it work before AVS?

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:14.000
Before AVS, verifying a beneficiary's assets often involved manual checks,

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:17.360
physical documents, and just a really high margin for human error,

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:19.680
or even intentional fraud.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.520
That sounds like a nightmare to audit.

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:23.760
It is. But AVS automates this.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:28.560
It pings financial institutions electronically to verify that a potential beneficiary's

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.400
liquid assets actually match what they report on their application.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.240
Okay. So they met the federal mandate.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:36.800
But meeting the mandate is one thing.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:40.080
The actual results are what really caught my attention in the source material.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:41.680
We have to talk about the PRM.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:43.520
Oh, the payment error rate measurement, yes.

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:48.480
Right. This is the single most important data point in Puerto Rico's compliance arsenal.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:52.320
Absolutely. The PRM measures the exact percentage

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:55.680
of a Medicaid program's payments that are improper.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:58.560
So whether that means paying the wrong amount,

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:00.800
paying for a service that wasn't provided,

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:03.440
or paying for an ineligible beneficiary.

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:05.120
And just to set the baseline for you,

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:08.720
the national average error rate for Medicaid across the United States

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:10.960
is 5.09 percent.

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:12.480
Right. Over 5 percent.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:16.160
But Puerto Rico's error rate came in at 2.51 percent.

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:17.760
Wait, let's make sure you heard that right.

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:20.080
Not only did Puerto Rico meet the federal compliance rules,

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:23.200
their error rate is actually half the national average.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:24.480
That's wild, isn't it?

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:28.960
That completely upends the usual narrative about territory administration.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:31.440
It really does. Think about it.

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:35.440
A territory operating under the constant stress of temporary funding,

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:39.600
dealing with the administrative chaos of looming fiscal cliffs,

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:42.560
somehow managed to achieve an error rate

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:45.120
that is literally half the national average.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.000
That requires incredibly tight procurement controls.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:51.520
And aggressive fraud prevention and seamless data management?

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:54.800
It totally dismantles that historical narrative.

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:59.840
You just cannot successfully argue that a jurisdiction lacks the administrative rigor

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:03.200
to handle permanent funding when they are demonstrably outperforming

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:05.040
the vast majority of fully funded states.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.600
Right. Okay, but let's play devil's advocate for a second here.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:08.080
Sure.

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:13.920
Having a 2.51 percent error rate and the CRS rulebook on your side is fantastic on paper.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:18.320
But none of that matters if Washington politicians can use local infighting

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:20.480
as an excuse to just delay everything.

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:24.800
I mean, if a hospital CEO goes to a senator asking for a specific reimbursement tweak

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:27.360
and then an insurance lobbyist goes to a house committee

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:29.120
asking for something else, what happens?

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:30.240
The bill dies.

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:34.160
Fractured lobbying is the easiest way to kill legislation in Congress.

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:35.680
Because it gives them an out.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:40.480
Exactly. When lawmakers hear conflicting requests from the exact same jurisdiction,

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:43.840
it gives them the perfect political cover to do absolutely nothing.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:45.680
They'll just look at the competing factions and say,

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:49.520
look, go back to San Juan, figure out what you actually want,

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:51.360
and come back to us when you agree.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:54.800
And by the time they figure it out, the 12-month clock has run out

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:56.800
and the whole system falls off the cliff.

00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:00.160
Which is exactly why the local political strategy had to evolve.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:04.320
And that leads us to Executive Order 2026-006.

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:08.960
Governor Jennifer Gonzalez signed this on February 5th, 2026.

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:13.840
Yes. And this order forcefully solved that fractured lobbying problem

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:16.160
by creating the multi-sectoral working group.

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:17.920
So who is actually in this group?

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:21.280
It brings together the Department of Health, the local Medicaid agency,

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:26.720
AZS, PRFAA in Washington, the Hospital Association,

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:30.960
pharmacies, insurers, medical associations, and public policy experts.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:32.160
All at the same table.

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:34.320
Okay, getting them in a room is one thing,

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:38.960
but why is it so crucial that the hospital CEO and the government official

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:42.160
walk into a congressional office with the exact same spreadsheet?

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:45.520
Like, how do you actually get historically competing interests,

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:47.760
hospitals who want higher payouts,

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:52.640
and insurers who want tighter margins to agree on a single legislative package

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:54.560
without it turning into a turf war?

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:58.160
By establishing a strict legal framework through the governor's mandate,

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:02.640
the Executive Order explicitly demands a one-voice approach.

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:03.440
One voice.

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:06.720
Right. It effectively tells the local healthcare ecosystem

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:09.920
that they are facing a mutually assured destruction scenario.

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:13.920
If the hospitals and the insurers fight over the minor details,

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:18.880
the September 2027 cliff hits everyone, and the entire system bleeds out.

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:21.200
So survival essentially forces consensus.

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:24.080
They had to put their individual sector lobbying aside

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:26.960
to agree on a unified macro level ask.

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:28.960
And they locked in a very specific set of numbers

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:31.200
so Congress doesn't hear conflicting requests.

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:36.720
The unified ask is $4.415 billion for fiscal year 2028.

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:38.800
$4.415 billion.

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:44.320
Yes, and that baseline includes a built-in 5% annual growth rate

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:46.080
to account for medical inflation.

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:46.800
Makes sense.

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:51.600
And most crucially, it demands parity with the other territories.

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:54.480
A permanent 83% FMAP.

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:56.960
So the line in the sand is permanence.

00:13:56.960 --> 00:13:59.120
They are not asking for another temporary patch.

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:06.480
They are asking for $4.415 billion, a 5% index, and an 83% match for ever.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:07.520
That's the mandate.

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:08.320
One voice.

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:11.440
Okay, so we have the indisputable facts from the CRS.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:15.440
We have the stellar 2.5, 1% compliance report card.

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:18.160
And we have a unified one voice coalition

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:20.560
demanding a specific permanent fix.

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:21.360
Right.

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:24.800
So how does this actually play out in Washington over the next 12 months?

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:26.720
I mean, what is the step-by-step path here?

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:30.240
It requires a highly choreographed seasonal legislative campaign.

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:35.280
It is a 12-month sprint mapped exactly to the rhythms of the congressional calendar.

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:36.960
It sounds like they aren't just asking for money.

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:40.160
They are running a highly choreographed military campaign.

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:41.760
That's the best way to describe it.

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:44.800
So we are currently in the fall 2026 phase, right?

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:48.160
Which runs from now through January 2027.

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:50.800
What's the immediate tactical goal right now?

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:52.400
Physical delivery and education.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:59.840
The multi-sectoral group is taking those Allison Mitchell CRS briefs and the compliance scorecard

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:02.720
and placing them directly into the hands of the staffers

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:05.600
on the House Energy and Commerce Incentive Finance Committees.

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:07.520
So they're building the baseline of facts.

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:08.000
Exactly.

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:13.280
The goal is to get a discussion draft of the legislation circulating before Thanksgiving.

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:19.680
But there is a massive political trap they have to avoid during this fall drafting phase.

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:20.560
The status debate.

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.080
Yes, the status debate.

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:26.080
If this Medicaid funding push gets tangled up in ideological arguments

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:29.600
about whether Puerto Rico should be a state or an independent nation,

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:31.440
the legislation will instantly stall.

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:33.360
So they have to keep it entirely separate.

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:34.560
Complete discipline.

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:38.320
It must be treated purely as an urgent health care financing crisis,

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:41.280
totally separate from the broader political status of the island.

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:44.080
Okay, keep the objective focused on health care.

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:45.840
That takes us through the winter.

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:48.000
What triggers in spring 2027?

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:49.440
So January through April.

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:50.880
Spring is markup season.

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:55.680
This is when the committees will actually debate the specifics of those discussion drafts.

00:15:56.240 --> 00:16:01.680
And this is when the multisexual group is going to aggressively push for official CBO scores

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:05.920
on their $4.415 billion package.

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:09.360
Because lawmakers won't vote on a massive permanent funding bill

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:13.360
until the Congressional Budget Office officially scores its long-term cost.

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:15.120
Right. You need the CBO score.

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:18.480
But there is also a critical external pressure point

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:21.200
that has to activate during these spring markups.

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:22.080
The FOMB.

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:24.240
Yes, the Financial Oversight and Management Board.

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:25.440
They have to step in.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:26.960
And they play a vital role here,

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:29.920
but purely regarding the Medicaid funding impact rate.

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:30.880
Exactly.

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:34.080
The board is responsible for certifying Puerto Rico's balanced budgets.

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:37.600
If Congress allows that September 2027 cliff to happen,

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:42.720
it triggers an immediate $3.5 billion shortfall in federal health care funding.

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:46.080
Just to be incredibly precise on this point for you listening,

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:50.800
we are strictly talking about the impact of the Medicaid cut on the certified budget.

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:51.280
Right.

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:54.320
We are not talking about audited financial statements of the government.

00:16:54.320 --> 00:16:56.480
This is purely about the Medicaid funding.

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:00.720
If the federal FMAP snaps back to 55%,

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:07.600
the local government cannot magically generate $3.5 billion in local revenue to fill that void.

00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:09.200
The math simply breaks.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:14.080
And the FOMB has to officially warn Congress of that reality in the spring.

00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:18.080
They must put it in writing that failing to fix the Medicaid cliff

00:17:18.080 --> 00:17:20.480
physically breaks the certified balanced budgets.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:24.160
It adds a whole layer of systemic fiscal panic to the health care crisis.

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:24.480
It does.

00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:26.480
It pushes us into the end game.

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:29.920
Summer 2027, May through September.

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:30.960
The final countdown.

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:36.240
The primary goal for the summer phase is to force a vote on a standalone Medicaid bill by Memorial Day.

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:39.920
So if you have the CBO score and the FOMB warning in hand,

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:42.320
Memorial Day is the target for standalone passage.

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:47.440
But relying on a standalone bill in the current congressional client is a massive gamble.

00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:48.080
Oh yeah.

00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:50.240
You always need a contingency plan.

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:51.120
And they have one.

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:55.200
If there is no standalone traction by Memorial Day,

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:57.840
the One Voice Coalition immediately pivots.

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:03.600
They shift their entire focus to attaching a Medicaid writer onto a must-pass September vehicle.

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:08.240
Because Congress inevitably has to pass some form of continuing resolution

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:12.560
or massive omnibus funding bill by the end of September just to prevent a federal government

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:13.200
shutdown.

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:14.000
Exactly.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:18.480
The strategy is to hitch the Puerto Rico Medicaid fix to that moving train.

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:18.960
Right.

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:21.840
And in that final desperate scenario in September,

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:24.960
the absolute minimum acceptable outcome is a freeze.

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:31.040
So if they can't get the permanent 83% FMAP passed in the chaos of a government funding fight,

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:35.280
they must at least secure a legislative freeze to maintain current funding levels.

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:35.840
Yes.

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:37.360
Prevent the cliff from triggering.

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:41.760
Keep the 76% patch for now while they continue to battle for permanence.

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:42.320
Wow.

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:44.800
It really is a grueling 12-month gauntlet.

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:47.760
Every single month has a specific tactical imperative.

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:50.480
And any mixed messaging from the local health care industry

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:54.880
could cause the entire fragile coalition on Capitol Hill to just collapse.

00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:56.560
The discipline required is immense.

00:18:56.560 --> 00:18:58.480
I mean they cannot afford a single misstep.

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:02.960
But the architecture of their argument is structurally very sound.

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:04.880
Let's synthesize what this all means.

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:09.120
Puerto Rico is staring down a September 30, 2027 cliff.

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:14.480
To defuse it, they are operating on an exact 12-month timeline.

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:14.800
Right.

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:17.120
They aren't relying on emotional appeals.

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:20.960
They are relying on the nonpartisan facts established by the CRS,

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:23.200
specifically Allison Mitchell's reports,

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:27.120
which prove the disparity between Puerto Rico's temporary patch

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:31.200
and the permanent 83% match enjoyed by the other territory.

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:34.720
And they are backing up those facts with empirical proof of compliance.

00:19:35.120 --> 00:19:36.240
They did the homework.

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:40.880
They overhauled their program integrity to meet the strict mandates of the CAA 2023.

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:41.120
Right.

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:43.520
They implemented the asset verification system

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:47.280
and achieved that incredible 2.5%, 1% PURM rate

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:50.080
that drastically outpaces the national average.

00:19:50.080 --> 00:19:52.080
And through the governor's executive order,

00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:55.760
they have silenced the usual fractured lobbying.

00:19:55.760 --> 00:19:59.120
They forced hospitals, insurers, and government agencies

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:06.800
to speak with one voice to demand a permanent $4.415 billion solution.

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:10.720
So the multi-sectoral group's message to Congress is essentially this.

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:12.720
We've done the heavy lifting.

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:14.240
We've hit the metrics you demanded,

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:16.320
and we have brought you a unified front.

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:20.960
Now it is up to Congress to provide permanent state-like funding

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:25.600
for the 1.3 million American citizens in Puerto Rico who rely on it

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:29.040
rather than pushing them toward another manufactured fiscal cliff.

00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:30.000
That's exactly it.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.240
The question now is whether Capitol Hill will accept

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:35.200
that empirical argument and grant parity impermanence

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:38.400
or just allow the 12-month countdown clock to hit zero.

